          "text": "Now I have the complete picture. Key chain details:\n\n- Fetch: Selenium → `../data/raw/dromic-new/{year}/` (manifest per year dir)\n- dromic-docx-to-pdf.py: converts DOCX to PDF → `../data/raw/dromic-new/{year}-pdf` (presumably)\n- parse/dromic.py: reads `../data/raw/dromic-new/{year}-pdf`, manifest.json alongside (from the raw dir? manifest_path = pdf_path.parent / \"manifest.json\" — in the -pdf dir; the converter probably copies the manifest), writes `../data/parsed/dromic/{year}/{event_folder}/` with metadata.json, source.json, CSVs (affected/displaced/served/house/assistance).\n- transform + run_dromic: per folder → triples → dromic-{year}.ttl → SHACL → GraphDB PUT.\n\nNow write up:\n\n1. **DROMIC assessment** — current pipeline strengths/gaps. The critical gap: scraper skips updated posts (scraped_urls by post_url) and grabs only the first PDF per post → misses all subsequent reports (#2, #3...). DROMIC posts accumulate many report versions — e.g., Mayon post has 110 reports. Each report is a snapshot (affected numbers change daily). The KG gets only the snapshot at first scrape. That explains \"data just couldn't be updated.\"\n\n2. **Automation approach** — RSS-based fetcher:\n   - WordPress feed is OPEN (verified): `https://dromic.dswd.gov.ph/category/situation-reports/{year}/feed/`\n   - Feed items contain: title (event + date + [Terminated]), permalink, pubDate, **WP categories = disaster type** (bonus: free disaster classification for DROMIC, no LLM), and full HTML content with ALL file links.\n   - New fetcher: requests + feedparser, per-file tracking keyed by file URL, downloads only new PDFs/DOCXs, updates manifest with report-number/date parsed from filename. ~100 lines, no Chrome, runnable in CI.\n   - Note `make_direct_download_link` handles Google viewer URLs — feed has both direct wp-content links and docs.google viewer iframe; the wp-content direct links are in `wp-block-file` divs — parseable by regex on href.\n   - Scheduli